// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `b` in a call to `len`
// THE ALIAS-APPLICATION HALF OF #1473, LEFT OPEN — and it was check-clean INVALID WASM.
//
// `Box<T>` at a FuncDecl type PARAMETER is a generic ORIGINAL's annotation. It denotes no
// concrete struct, so `gaeEnsure` must intern nothing: the original is replaced by its
// instances and each instance re-runs the pass over its PINNED name (`Box<i32>`), which is
// concrete and interns correctly.
//
// Without the skip, `gaeApplyFieldTy` substituted the parameter FOR ITSELF — the alias body
// `X[]` under `X := T` is `T[]` — and the row interned with the type parameter read as a
// NOMINAL element name, exactly the root #1473 closed for the INLINE spelling
// `function len<T>(b: { v: T[] })`. That inline twin is a loud reject; this one was
// `vl check --codegen` rc 0 and `vl run` "expected (ref null $type), found i32".
//
// The asymmetry is the one `genTyParamArr`'s own header names one layer up — a classifier
// taught half of a set its sibling already had whole. #1473 taught the inline half.
//
// THE SHADOW RULING IS PRESERVED AND IS WHY THE GUARD IS NOT JUST `fnTyParamName`. A
// parameter that also names a real type binds nothing, and then the application IS
// concrete and must still intern. `genapp-tyaram-arg-shadow-control.vl` is that program
// and it must keep printing 1; the guard asks `genTyParamArr`'s own accept-set test of the
// ARRAY spelling so the two halves cannot drift.
type Box<X> = { v: X[] }

function len<T>(b: Box<T>): i32 {
  b.v.length
}

print(len({ v: [1, 2, 3] }))
